; Tutorial_2_Install_and_Launch.inix -- a spoken walk through BatCon, written for buildTutorials (the format is
; described at the top of Tutorial_0_Overview.inix in the Homer kit's Templates).

[about]
Title=2 - Install and Launch
Intro=Installing BatCon, what the installer offers, and opening the program.
Setup=A web browser is open, and your reader is running.
Homework=Install BatCon, then open it with its desktop key.

[step]
Say=BatCon installs from its page on GitHub, from a single file, BatCon underscore setup dot E X E. Downloading it and pressing Enter on it starts the installer.

[step]
Say=Windows first asks whether to let the installer make changes, since BatCon installs for every user of the computer. Alt plus Y, Yankee, answers yes.
Key=Alt+Y
Hear=Setup - BatCon dialog

[step]
Say=The installer asks a few questions, and each has the usual answer ready, so Enter moves you on. The first is where to install; the usual place is the Program Files folder.
Key=Enter
Hear=Select Destination Location

[step]
Say=Keep pressing Enter through the questions. When the files are copied, the last page lists the extras BatCon can use, each as a check box, ticked when it is needed.
Key=Enter
Hear=Completing the BatCon Setup Wizard

[step]
Say=The extras are tools BatCon converts with. Pandoc and the PDF tools turn documents into other documents. ffmpeg handles sound and video. Whisper writes down what is said in a recording, on your own computer.

[step]
Say=A box already ticked means the tool is missing or out of date. A box not ticked means it is already there and current. Enter installs whatever is ticked.
Key=Enter
Hear=BatCon Setup

[step]
Say=When the installer finishes, a Results box says what it did with each tool: installed, updated, or already current. Down Arrow reads it a line at a time, and Enter closes it.
Key=Enter
Note=The reader says nothing as the box closes; the installer has finished.

[step]
Say=To install with no questions at all, start the installer with slash default after its name. It then takes every usual answer by itself, as if you had pressed Enter on each screen.

[step]
Say=Now open BatCon with its desktop key, Alt plus Control plus B. It works from anywhere in Windows, whatever program you are in.
Key=Alt+Control+B
Hear=BatCon dialog
Hear=Source files: edit, Documents

[step]
Say=BatCon is also on the Start menu. The Windows key, then the word BatCon, then Enter, opens it the same way.
Key=Windows
Hear=Search box

[step]
Say=Choices are kept between runs only when Use configuration is checked as you press OK; otherwise BatCon starts fresh each time, from its defaults.
Key=Alt+U
Hear=Use configuration, check box, not checked

[step]
Say=Updating later is the same: run the newest setup file, and it installs over the old one, keeping your settings. Inside BatCon, F11 checks for a newer version, says whether there is one, and offers to install it.

[step]
Say=If a tool on the finish page could not be installed, the Results box says why, and BatCon still works for every conversion that does not need it. A conversion that does need it is reported as failed, with the tool named.

[step]
Say=Uninstalling is from Windows Settings, under Apps, like any program; Windows plus I opens Settings. Your saved settings and logs stay in your own folder until you remove them.
Key=Windows+I
Hear=Settings

[step]
Say=What this walk taught: where to get BatCon, that the installer offers the tools BatCon needs, slash default for an install with no questions, and Alt plus Control plus B to open it.
